I finally got a human convict down to the quest, without resorting to
any kind of cheating.  When I arrived there, this is what I got:
> "Couldn't load "@ R " - making a maze.
> Program in disorder - perhaps you'd better #quit.

The program gave me a maze level then.  It seems that the proper files
(Con-????.lev) weren't installed – maybe left out somewhere in the
Makefile?  A real pity, it had been my best convict game so far.

In the meantime, I've compiled the stand-alone level compiler and added
the convict levels to the playground; a quick test in wizard mode
confirmed that indeed the first quest level loads.  But just going down
the staircase then didn't work on the original game since I wasn't
granted permission for the quest (I'd have been ready), neither did
jumping down a hole or trap door.

Another minor item:  I discovered a striped shirt on the floor
somewhere; the description said T-Shirt.  Probably my own fault; these
two tiles should be switched.


On the game itself:

Survived the early game.  (With the new adjustments, it is now much
easier to avoid starving early, i.e. less dependent on RNG luck.  A
point for inclusion in the mainstream distribution, whichever it will
be.)  Caution and prayers got me through the first three floors, where I
found the entrance to the mines.

The first and second mines levels were lit – that got me the armor I so
desperately needed.  The second one had a magic lamp too, which I
promptly converted to Mjollnir, dropping the iron ball.  The daggers
found on these two floors lasted for the rest of the game.  With that
and AC -4, I felt safe enough to take out the mine watch, and promptly
did so.  Mine town had a second magic lamp, which I used as a permanent
light source from that point on (a good thing for humans in general).
After I saw that there were exactly 7 candles, I left the rest of the
mines for later, but somehow never got to it.

I descended the main dungeon cautiously.  A number of chaotic altars
gave me (at different points) Luck Blade (never used it, since I already
had Mjollnir), Ogresmasher, and Fire Brand.  I used Fire Brand just
enough to get longsword to basic, then switched back to Mjollnir.  A
beehive on level 10 gave me 10 lumps of royal jelly for additional
carrying capacity.

The top Sokoban level had the BoH I was quite desperate for at that
point.  A close call at the entrance to the zoo there; a wand of
teleportation (used on the monsters) and then a convenient chickatrice
corpse saved me there.

Further down; a large throne room with a sleeping crowd provided much
random loot but no special effects.  An early tengu granted teleport
control; a leprechaun soon after that point made me feel jumpy enough to
use it.  I had most of the essential properties at that point, except
for reflection and magic resistance.  Tymora had already revealed the
Castle pass tune to me, and I had a magic flute from mine town.  Two
wraiths in the big room prepared me for the quest – and that was it then.


Some general points on the convict:

Generally, I find the role quite interesting and more challenging than
any other I have tried.  I have experience with wizards, priests, and
tourists, but that game was different enough.

I found enough spellbooks (three level 1 spells, three spellbooks of
cure sickness which the convict doesn't need at all, and several
interesting spells) but wasn't yet ready to drop my AC to use them.  It
would have been one of the next moves to get some DSM, maybe using the
magic lamp.  Magic mostly seems to be a late-game option for the
convict.  I'd probably have used magic missile and magic mapping
frequently, both of which I had ready for use.  I also found cone of
cold and fireball, but I don't know if the convict can use them
effectively.  Didn't find identify, but the scrolls were sufficient here.

Due to the high number of co-aligned altars (4 in total), I didn't have
any problems getting holy water, and BUC-identification was easy enough.
 So I played the game a bit like a priest, but this certainly can't be
done in all convict games.  Even though I didn't have enough money to
buy protection (I'd have had it if I'd done a bit more mining), I
somehow didn't yet dare to use the single non-chaotic altar to create
unholy water.  Didn't find that on the floor either.

The tourist is probably most similar to the convict in the middle game,
simply because he is a less specialized role then most.  Still, he
starts with some quite useful stuff, and therefore the early game
differs much.  The tourist can proceed slowly and retreat often, while
the Convict is driven down by hunger.  Also, the skill slots ask for
different strategies.  Without Mjollnir, I'd probably have gone for a
mattock or, otherwise, maybe I'd have tried the crysknife.  I've never
given that much thought, so far – maybe I'm wrong here.  Didn't drop the
iron ball much before getting Mjollnir.

Even with Mjollnir, the HPs still didn't make it possible to play the
convict warrior-style – retreat and use of the stack of (skilled)
daggers is important as well.  Generally, I find the patch well-balanced
now.

